# ============================================================================
# AGB HEURISTICS - Suricata behavioral/heuristic detection rules (agent-side)
# ============================================================================
# Unlike agb-black.rules (confirmed-bad IOCs) and agb-white.rules (known-good
# suppressions), these are PATTERN/BEHAVIOR heuristics - they catch categories
# of suspicious activity the stock ET Open ruleset and agb-black.rules don't
# cover: DGA-style domains, large outbound transfers, and known-bad JA3 TLS
# fingerprints. All stay action `alert` (never auto-converted to `drop` by
# build-suricata-ips.ps1, same treatment as suricata.rules) - heuristics have
# a real false-positive rate and should never auto-block without a human
# reviewing first. sid range 1000200+ reserved for this file.
#
# Corresponding Wazuh manager escalation rules (101000-101059) are documented
# separately - see the org's Custom_Suricata.xml / local_rules_c2.xml.
# ============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DGA (Domain Generation Algorithm) heuristic
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Catches the general SHAPE of a DGA domain (long, single-label, no vowel-rich
# dictionary structure, no dashes) rather than any specific malware family.
# This is inherently approximate - legitimate CDN/cloud-storage endpoints
# (S3 buckets, some CDN edge hostnames) occasionally match this shape too,
# which is exactly why this fires an ALERT for a human to review, never a
# block. Tune false positives by adding known-good matches to agb-white.rules
# rather than editing this pattern.
alert dns any any -> any any (msg:"AGB HEURISTIC: Possible DGA domain query (long random-looking label)"; dns.query; pcre:"/^[a-z0-9]{16,40}\.(com|net|org|info|biz|top|xyz|club|online)$/i"; sid:1000200; rev:1;)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Large outbound transfer heuristic (possible data exfiltration)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Flags a single TCP flow that has sent more than ~50MB to a REMOTE
# (non-RFC1918) destination. Threshold is deliberately high to avoid
# flooding on ordinary large downloads/uploads (cloud backups, video calls,
# Windows Update) - tune $EXTERNAL_NET/threshold per environment if this is
# too noisy or not sensitive enough.
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> !$HOME_NET any (msg:"AGB HEURISTIC: Large outbound data transfer (possible exfiltration)"; flow:established,to_server; stream_size:client,>,52428800; sid:1000201; rev:1;)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JA3 TLS fingerprint blocklist (STARTER SET - expand as needed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JA3 hashes fingerprint the TLS ClientHello itself (cipher list, extensions,
# curves) - many malware families/C2 frameworks reuse the same TLS library
# defaults and so share a stable JA3 hash regardless of which domain/IP
# they're currently using, unlike IP/domain blocklists which go stale fast.
# REQUIRES suricata.yaml: app-layer.protocols.tls.ja3-fingerprints: yes
# (off by default - a measurable per-flow CPU cost, opt-in only). Without
# this yaml setting, these rules load fine but will never match anything.
# This is a tiny STARTER set (well-known public/historical hashes) - a real
# deployment should pull a maintained JA3 threat-intel feed and refresh it
# on the same daily schedule as agb-black.rules, not hand-maintain this list.
alert tls any any -> any any (msg:"AGB HEURISTIC: JA3 hash matches known Trickbot/Emotet-family loader"; ja3.hash; content:"6734f37431670b3ab4292b8f60f29984"; sid:1000210; rev:1;)
alert tls any any -> any any (msg:"AGB HEURISTIC: JA3 hash matches known generic malware TLS stack"; ja3.hash; content:"e7d705a3286e19ea42f587b344ee6865"; sid:1000211; rev:1;)

# Add future heuristics below, one alert rule per entry.
# Example (commented out) - unusually high DNS query VOLUME from one host in
# a short window (possible DNS tunneling) is better done as a Wazuh
# frequency/timeframe correlation rule on event_type:dns than a single
# Suricata signature - see the manager-side rules for that pattern instead.
